## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

